1
00:00:00,476 --> 00:00:01,166
Hello, everyone.

2
00:00:01,543 --> 00:00:04,911
I'm Prateek Jangal, a staff
software engineer at Confluent.

3
00:00:05,446 --> 00:00:10,357
My work focuses on distributed systems,
cloud infrastructure, and the control

4
00:00:10,366 --> 00:00:15,640
panes that help large-scale platforms
operate reliably Today, I will

5
00:00:15,648 --> 00:00:19,556
discuss building self-healing cloud
infrastructure with feature flags.

6
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,183
Feature flags are usually associated
with application releases, enabling

7
00:00:26,183 --> 00:00:30,686
a feature for a small percentage of
users and then gradually expanding it.

8
00:00:32,527 --> 00:00:38,152
But what if we use them as infrastructure
controls, allowing systems to

9
00:00:38,155 --> 00:00:43,257
change routing, disable unstable
behavior, activate fallbacks, and

10
00:00:43,257 --> 00:00:44,952
recover from predictable failures?

11
00:00:46,649 --> 00:00:52,434
The goal is not to eliminate failures,
it is to detect them early, contain their

12
00:00:52,437 --> 00:00:59,047
impact, recover safely, and preserve human
control when the situation is ambiguous.

13
00:01:00,890 --> 00:01:05,840
Let's start with why traditional
operations struggle at scale The central

14
00:01:05,840 --> 00:01:10,335
idea is that self-healing should be
a feedback loop, not a collection

15
00:01:10,336 --> 00:01:12,559
of unrelated scripts or commands.

16
00:01:13,994 --> 00:01:18,726
We observe the system detect a
problem, decide what action is

17
00:01:18,728 --> 00:01:24,988
appropriate, recover, and verify the
result Feature flags can provide a

18
00:01:24,989 --> 00:01:30,342
reversible mechanism for changing
system behavior within this very loop

19
00:01:31,342 --> 00:01:36,228
Traditional operations follow a
similar sequence: alert, investigate,

20
00:01:36,488 --> 00:01:38,504
diagnose, and manually remediate.

21
00:01:39,950 --> 00:01:44,412
That works for unusual incidents,
but it becomes expensive when

22
00:01:44,430 --> 00:01:48,358
predictable failures repeatedly
require the same intervention.

23
00:01:50,275 --> 00:01:55,011
Consider a distributed service that
depends on several downstream layers.

24
00:01:56,144 --> 00:02:02,737
One dependency becomes unhealthy, retries
increase, latency rises, and the problem

25
00:02:02,743 --> 00:02:08,031
spreads to other services If we already
understand the failure and have a safe

26
00:02:08,057 --> 00:02:13,210
mitigation plan, should an engineer really
have to intervene every single time?

27
00:02:14,029 --> 00:02:14,321
No.

28
00:02:14,327 --> 00:02:14,712
Right?

29
00:02:16,352 --> 00:02:18,990
Self-healing automates
known recovery paths.

30
00:02:19,536 --> 00:02:24,277
The important qualification is that
the automation needs clear boundaries.

31
00:02:24,973 --> 00:02:29,002
It should not take arbitrary
actions just because a metric

32
00:02:29,007 --> 00:02:33,806
crossed a threshold So what should
a self-healing system look like?

33
00:02:34,806 --> 00:02:36,969
Think of this as a feedback control loop.

34
00:02:37,285 --> 00:02:43,123
You observe, detect, decide,
recover, and observe again.

35
00:02:44,717 --> 00:02:49,057
The final step matters the
most, and it's most important.

36
00:02:49,978 --> 00:02:54,233
Successfully executing a command
or a script does not prove

37
00:02:54,312 --> 00:02:55,735
that the system recovered.

38
00:02:56,854 --> 00:03:01,234
For example, restarting a service
may succeed while its downstream

39
00:03:01,235 --> 00:03:02,787
dependency remains unhealthy.

40
00:03:04,563 --> 00:03:09,338
Without validation, the automation
could report success while users

41
00:03:09,370 --> 00:03:11,364
continue to experience failures.

42
00:03:12,943 --> 00:03:16,922
Every recovery action should
therefore have an expected outcome

43
00:03:17,418 --> 00:03:21,912
and a way to measure whether that
outcome actually occurred or not

44
00:03:22,912 --> 00:03:25,882
Therefore, I separate the
architecture in s- five separate

45
00:03:25,884 --> 00:03:28,897
layers First, observability.

46
00:03:29,322 --> 00:03:33,449
It includes metrics, logs,
traces, and flag change events.

47
00:03:34,142 --> 00:03:35,331
Then comes detection.

48
00:03:36,244 --> 00:03:39,317
Converting this telemetry
into actionable conditions.

49
00:03:40,088 --> 00:03:40,979
And then decision.

50
00:03:41,622 --> 00:03:44,557
Evaluating whether intervention
is justified or not.

51
00:03:45,335 --> 00:03:46,252
And then recovery.

52
00:03:46,828 --> 00:03:50,495
Performing a bounded action,
such as disabling a capability

53
00:03:50,762 --> 00:03:52,128
or redirecting traffic.

54
00:03:52,908 --> 00:03:55,056
And at the end, validation.

55
00:03:55,357 --> 00:03:57,632
Confirming that health improved.

56
00:03:59,502 --> 00:04:02,917
Keeping these responsibilities
separate prevents every alert

57
00:04:02,923 --> 00:04:04,589
from becoming an automatic action.

58
00:04:06,253 --> 00:04:08,480
Detection tells us
something may be very wrong.

59
00:04:08,974 --> 00:04:11,787
Decision determines whether
we should act or not.

60
00:04:12,376 --> 00:04:15,852
Recovery changes the system,
and then validation determines

61
00:04:16,155 --> 00:04:18,062
whether that change helped or not

62
00:04:19,062 --> 00:04:20,924
Now, this is the central idea of the talk.

63
00:04:21,950 --> 00:04:26,339
A feature flag is a fundamentally
runtime control decision.

64
00:04:28,264 --> 00:04:30,691
It does not have to control
a user-facing feature.

65
00:04:31,221 --> 00:04:35,905
It can select a routing policy,
caching strategy, fallback dependency,

66
00:04:35,905 --> 00:04:39,073
processing path, experimental
optimization, and whatnot.

67
00:04:40,225 --> 00:04:43,610
Imagine a service introducing
a new routing strategy.

68
00:04:44,882 --> 00:04:49,493
Instead of making that strategy active
for each and every request, we can keep

69
00:04:49,494 --> 00:04:55,366
it disabled by default and activate
it for it-- for a limited scope If the

70
00:04:55,370 --> 00:05:00,354
behavior is unhealthy, we can disable
that specific path without having to

71
00:05:00,355 --> 00:05:03,621
necessarily redeploy the entire service.

72
00:05:05,435 --> 00:05:10,666
The key point being, feature flags
can provide a control mechanism for

73
00:05:10,711 --> 00:05:17,209
infrastructure behavior separate from the
mechanism that deploys the very software

74
00:05:18,209 --> 00:05:22,015
See, now deployment and activation
are two very different operations.

75
00:05:22,529 --> 00:05:26,413
First, deploy the code while
keeping the new behavior intact.

76
00:05:26,967 --> 00:05:33,063
Next, activate it for the restricted scope
and monitor the relevant health signals

77
00:05:34,063 --> 00:05:36,151
If it remains healthy, expand the scope.

78
00:05:36,336 --> 00:05:38,175
If it degrades, you deactivate it.

79
00:05:39,165 --> 00:05:42,911
Now, this separation can reduce
recovery time because we may not need

80
00:05:42,911 --> 00:05:44,826
to roll back the entire deployment.

81
00:05:45,655 --> 00:05:49,710
It also makes your releases faster
and releases easier to reason about.

82
00:05:50,307 --> 00:05:52,884
Code can be present in an
environment without immediately

83
00:05:52,885 --> 00:05:55,716
affecting anything, anything at all.

84
00:05:56,527 --> 00:06:03,452
The caveat is that the old path has to
remain safe, it has to remain usable, and

85
00:06:03,452 --> 00:06:07,334
the flag must be evaluated consistently
wherever the behavior is controlled.

86
00:06:08,910 --> 00:06:12,706
It's very important that we do
backward compatibility checks.

87
00:06:12,784 --> 00:06:17,135
In event of any issue, when you roll
back your part of the deployment,

88
00:06:18,469 --> 00:06:20,569
the prior change should still work

89
00:06:21,569 --> 00:06:26,067
Now suppose a new capability is activated
and latency increases a few minutes later.

90
00:06:27,302 --> 00:06:30,421
The system should correlate the
degradation with a recent flag

91
00:06:30,424 --> 00:06:35,313
change, then evaluate whether the
evidence is strong enough and if

92
00:06:35,340 --> 00:06:39,711
the configured conditions are met,
disable the capability automatically.

93
00:06:41,305 --> 00:06:44,531
Then it must check whether the
health has actually recovered or not.

94
00:06:45,174 --> 00:06:49,409
This requires integrating flag
with system observability.

95
00:06:51,135 --> 00:06:55,508
Flag changes should generate telemetry,
and monitoring should have enough

96
00:06:55,539 --> 00:07:01,521
context to connect behavioral changes
with subsequent outcomes Correlation

97
00:07:01,547 --> 00:07:06,791
alone doesn't always prove causation,
so triggers should be designed

98
00:07:06,791 --> 00:07:11,414
carefully to avoid reversing healthy
changes because of unrelated incidents

99
00:07:12,414 --> 00:07:18,138
Now, key reliability principle is to
limit the change to the smallest practical

100
00:07:18,147 --> 00:07:22,296
scope, and this is very important
to keep your blast radius, small.

101
00:07:23,156 --> 00:07:27,008
Instead of activating a new
behavior everywhere, scope it

102
00:07:27,635 --> 00:07:29,636
to a limited set of, users.

103
00:07:29,762 --> 00:07:33,817
Like you can scope it to limited
set of environment, service,

104
00:07:33,831 --> 00:07:35,877
cluster, region, or tenant.

105
00:07:36,686 --> 00:07:41,064
You start small, you observe real
production behavior, whether it is

106
00:07:41,119 --> 00:07:45,956
doing well or whether it is having
some issues, and you expand only

107
00:07:45,957 --> 00:07:48,427
when the evidence supports doing so.

108
00:07:50,176 --> 00:07:51,661
Pre-production testing is essential.

109
00:07:51,669 --> 00:07:53,376
Yes, you have to test it.

110
00:07:53,689 --> 00:07:58,715
Without that, you cannot proceed forward,
but it cannot perfectly reproduce every

111
00:07:58,715 --> 00:08:03,438
production traffic pattern or customer
interaction or dependency interaction,

112
00:08:03,518 --> 00:08:05,244
any customer-specific workload.

113
00:08:06,150 --> 00:08:10,484
So progressive activation gives
us a controlled way to learn from

114
00:08:10,487 --> 00:08:14,590
real conditions without exposing
the entire fleet simultaneously

115
00:08:15,590 --> 00:08:18,792
Now it is very important that
we do graceful degradation

116
00:08:18,897 --> 00:08:20,729
over a failure, right?

117
00:08:21,165 --> 00:08:26,466
Now, say consider an external dependency
that becomes unreliable, right?

118
00:08:26,483 --> 00:08:30,945
You might be, say, talking to, S3
or like DynamoDB, which you don't

119
00:08:30,947 --> 00:08:32,568
have any control over, right?

120
00:08:33,055 --> 00:08:37,343
Aggressive retries can amplify the
pressure on an already unhealthy system.

121
00:08:39,268 --> 00:08:42,962
If the application supports a
safe fallback, we may be able to

122
00:08:42,966 --> 00:08:47,558
disable the affected integration and
preserve essential functionality.

123
00:08:49,540 --> 00:08:54,310
The service operates in reduced mode
instead of failing completely, and I feel

124
00:08:54,323 --> 00:08:58,132
that is a very thought after compromise.

125
00:08:58,389 --> 00:09:01,377
Your customers can still interact
with your service or with your

126
00:09:01,377 --> 00:09:04,190
software, but at a reduced capability.

127
00:09:05,875 --> 00:09:08,758
But it may not be appropriate
for every qu-- operation.

128
00:09:09,104 --> 00:09:13,518
We must preserve data integrity and
the correctness of critical workloads.

129
00:09:13,819 --> 00:09:20,318
But whenever a degraded mode is safe,
making it explicit and controllable

130
00:09:20,357 --> 00:09:22,670
can substantially improve resiliency

131
00:09:23,670 --> 00:09:26,282
Now, how do you design a
safe automated remediation?

132
00:09:27,672 --> 00:09:32,532
See, automation can be wrong, and
it can be wrong very efficiently.

133
00:09:34,073 --> 00:09:38,031
And I like to apply
safe-- four safeguards.

134
00:09:38,666 --> 00:09:41,141
First, define clear trigger conditions.

135
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:43,382
Avoid reacting to transient fluctuations.

136
00:09:43,383 --> 00:09:47,603
If there's a spike, a transient spike,
say like for a minute or so, or for

137
00:09:47,703 --> 00:09:51,514
a short amount of duration, I don't
want to run my, remediation, right?

138
00:09:51,798 --> 00:09:54,477
So we should require a
sufficiently strong evidence.

139
00:09:54,890 --> 00:09:57,289
Second, you limit the remediation scope.

140
00:09:57,871 --> 00:10:01,134
Disable one capability
rather than disrupting entire

141
00:10:01,134 --> 00:10:02,538
deployment or entire fleet.

142
00:10:03,296 --> 00:10:05,241
And then we validate
each and every action.

143
00:10:06,249 --> 00:10:08,604
A successful command is
not a proof of recovery.

144
00:10:08,618 --> 00:10:13,864
You have to validate when the system got
restored or system is working correctly.

145
00:10:15,351 --> 00:10:19,466
And at the end, prevent endless
loops and preserve auditability.

146
00:10:19,467 --> 00:10:21,629
And I must say this is
really, really important.

147
00:10:22,302 --> 00:10:28,064
If repeated remediation attempts
fail, it's, it's not likely that

148
00:10:28,065 --> 00:10:29,588
the next attempt will succeed.

149
00:10:30,075 --> 00:10:33,242
At that moment, we should stop
and escalate to an engineer

150
00:10:33,250 --> 00:10:36,825
rather than repeating the same
action on and on, and on, and on.

151
00:10:37,949 --> 00:10:44,270
Record the condition, decision, action,
flag change, and resulting health state.

152
00:10:44,755 --> 00:10:48,579
This makes the system understandable
and helps engineers investigate

153
00:10:48,580 --> 00:10:50,189
failures in the automation itself

154
00:10:51,189 --> 00:10:55,161
Now, my guiding principle is
that you automate certainty

155
00:10:55,900 --> 00:10:57,292
and you escalate ambiguity.

156
00:10:57,440 --> 00:11:02,690
When you are in ambiguity, when you don't
know what to do, let it be on the experts,

157
00:11:02,691 --> 00:11:08,025
on the SMEs, on the engineers to take
action rather than your automated, scripts

158
00:11:08,027 --> 00:11:09,566
or your automations decide on that.

159
00:11:10,635 --> 00:11:14,035
See, predictable failures with
known safe mitigations are

160
00:11:14,058 --> 00:11:15,475
good candidates for automation.

161
00:11:16,298 --> 00:11:20,708
Unclear root causes, simultaneous
failures across multiple systems,

162
00:11:21,211 --> 00:11:26,041
security concerns, and data integrity
risks may require human judgment,

163
00:11:26,042 --> 00:11:27,696
and most of the time it does.

164
00:11:28,746 --> 00:11:32,831
Operators must be able to inspect
and override automated decisions.

165
00:11:33,080 --> 00:11:34,273
That is really, really important.

166
00:11:34,283 --> 00:11:38,742
You need to have a break glass mechanism
for each and every automation we do.

167
00:11:40,520 --> 00:11:42,933
Each operational flag
should also have an owner.

168
00:11:43,623 --> 00:11:46,612
See, a documented purpose,
a known default state, clear

169
00:11:46,616 --> 00:11:49,028
permissions, and a retirement plan.

170
00:11:49,474 --> 00:11:52,936
Otherwise, you can just keep
on accumulating flags, and it

171
00:11:52,953 --> 00:11:57,802
becomes a source of operational
risk and a major, major tech debt.

172
00:11:57,846 --> 00:12:02,558
I am telling you this from experience
that each flag needs to be scoped down,

173
00:12:03,162 --> 00:12:07,402
else you're to-- you're looking at them
after some time, and you don't know,

174
00:12:07,403 --> 00:12:10,967
like, what this flag does, who owns it,
and whether you can retire it or not

175
00:12:11,967 --> 00:12:13,538
So now let, let's connect all the pieces.

176
00:12:13,539 --> 00:12:15,233
We have talked about multiple things.

177
00:12:15,609 --> 00:12:18,298
So deploy the new path
while leaving it disabled.

178
00:12:18,791 --> 00:12:20,219
Activate it for a limited scope.

179
00:12:20,219 --> 00:12:23,965
Scope it to environment, scope it to
cluster, scope it to maybe even a set

180
00:12:23,967 --> 00:12:26,388
of users or availability zone, right?

181
00:12:26,688 --> 00:12:28,669
You detect and evaluate degradation.

182
00:12:28,768 --> 00:12:31,092
You use metrics, observability, telemetry.

183
00:12:31,437 --> 00:12:35,230
See, like, is your system performing
well or if, system is performing,

184
00:12:35,639 --> 00:12:37,427
not so well, it has degraded.

185
00:12:38,158 --> 00:12:42,123
If the configured condition
justify intervention, automatically

186
00:12:42,124 --> 00:12:44,068
disable the path, right?

187
00:12:44,366 --> 00:12:47,815
You don't want to disable, the path
on some transient blips, right?

188
00:12:48,407 --> 00:12:50,466
So wait for your conditions to be me-met.

189
00:12:51,191 --> 00:12:56,042
And at the end, very important, validate
the recovery and record the outcome.

190
00:12:56,755 --> 00:13:01,634
Each step has to be observable,
bounded, and reversible.

191
00:13:02,569 --> 00:13:07,173
Now, this gives an end-to-end workflow
rather than a disconnected collection of

192
00:13:07,221 --> 00:13:09,892
alerts, scripts, commands, and whatnot

193
00:13:10,892 --> 00:13:12,489
We build the confidence gradually.

194
00:13:13,265 --> 00:13:16,751
Organizations don't need to automate
everything right away, right?

195
00:13:17,096 --> 00:13:21,122
You start in detection-only mode,
identify problems, and recommend

196
00:13:21,123 --> 00:13:23,779
actions while engineers remain
responsible for remediation.

197
00:13:24,226 --> 00:13:27,145
For example, you, you put
in some alerts, right?

198
00:13:27,440 --> 00:13:31,437
And when your threshold crosses,
they just notify the engineer

199
00:13:31,439 --> 00:13:32,968
on call or engineer responsible.

200
00:13:33,424 --> 00:13:36,084
You write SOPs for them, you write
runbooks for them, and they can

201
00:13:36,085 --> 00:13:37,991
follow those steps to remediate.

202
00:13:39,170 --> 00:13:42,032
So next, then you automate
the low-risk cases.

203
00:13:42,093 --> 00:13:46,167
Maybe like some of the events, they
just require restarting a Kubernetes

204
00:13:46,192 --> 00:13:51,564
pod or restarting, your service, right,
which are low risk while keeping the

205
00:13:51,565 --> 00:13:53,489
critical decisions under human control.

206
00:13:54,909 --> 00:13:58,832
You expand the automation as
operational evidence builds confidence.

207
00:13:59,835 --> 00:14:02,910
and then you regularly test
the recovery mechanism itself.

208
00:14:03,804 --> 00:14:08,216
An untested recovery workflow is an
assumption, not a proven capability

209
00:14:09,216 --> 00:14:14,118
And then most important, security,
life cycle, and platform engineering.

210
00:14:15,120 --> 00:14:16,492
These areas do matter.

211
00:14:18,126 --> 00:14:18,876
Security.

212
00:14:19,217 --> 00:14:23,536
Automated actions need strong
authentication, authorization, audit

213
00:14:23,536 --> 00:14:25,759
logging, and least privilege permissions.

214
00:14:25,796 --> 00:14:28,373
Each and every script needs authorization.

215
00:14:29,658 --> 00:14:33,583
We cannot have anyone just
randomly run it, right?

216
00:14:33,851 --> 00:14:37,779
If, for example, if you're owning
service A, it has to be, it has to

217
00:14:37,779 --> 00:14:43,555
be that service A users or service
A developers have control whether a

218
00:14:43,555 --> 00:14:45,059
script is getting executed or not.

219
00:14:45,853 --> 00:14:48,636
If someone is trying to execute it,
it requires approvals from them.

220
00:14:50,113 --> 00:14:51,057
Then life cycles.

221
00:14:51,328 --> 00:14:55,635
Flags should progress from introduction
to controlled activation, stable

222
00:14:55,635 --> 00:14:57,184
operation, and then retirement.

223
00:14:57,981 --> 00:15:01,096
Permanent resilience flags
should be explicitly identified.

224
00:15:01,323 --> 00:15:04,281
It is very, very important that
each flag has a life cycle.

225
00:15:04,785 --> 00:15:10,659
Else, we run in this spiral of tech
debt, spiral of like unclear actions,

226
00:15:11,006 --> 00:15:13,575
and it becomes a nightmare to operate.

227
00:15:15,399 --> 00:15:16,835
Then platform engineering.

228
00:15:17,532 --> 00:15:22,182
Standardizing telemetry, health
reporting, remediation workflows,

229
00:15:22,220 --> 00:15:26,223
and recovery validation makes
this approach much easier to apply

230
00:15:26,261 --> 00:15:29,498
consistently across multiple services

231
00:15:30,498 --> 00:15:32,761
Now let me summarize the four principles.

232
00:15:33,354 --> 00:15:38,057
First, automate known failure paths
where safe remediation is understood.

233
00:15:39,468 --> 00:15:42,646
Then you make changes
reversible, so unstable behavior

234
00:15:42,658 --> 00:15:44,182
can be deactivated quickly.

235
00:15:45,508 --> 00:15:49,298
It is very important you limit the blast
radius through progressive targeting.

236
00:15:49,767 --> 00:15:53,979
And finally, escalate uncertainty
rather than allowing automation to make

237
00:15:54,250 --> 00:15:56,465
increasingly speculative decisions.

238
00:15:56,516 --> 00:15:57,845
It's very, very important

239
00:15:58,845 --> 00:16:02,573
The goal isn't to build a system
where nothing fails, and to be

240
00:16:02,573 --> 00:16:04,359
practical, that's not even possible.

241
00:16:04,386 --> 00:16:06,039
Each and every system fails.

242
00:16:06,797 --> 00:16:12,349
It is to build a system which is
resilient, where failures can occur

243
00:16:12,388 --> 00:16:17,976
without becoming major disruptions, and
that's called a very, very good software

244
00:16:18,976 --> 00:16:22,198
Now, towards the closing, if
you take away one idea from this

245
00:16:22,207 --> 00:16:24,931
talk, I would like it to be this.

246
00:16:25,555 --> 00:16:28,724
Feature flags don't have to
be only release mechanisms.

247
00:16:29,060 --> 00:16:31,994
They can be infrastructure
controls for you.

248
00:16:32,995 --> 00:16:38,860
Combined with observability, bounded
remediation, validation, progressive

249
00:16:38,916 --> 00:16:44,079
activation, and human governance, they can
become an important part of a self-healing

250
00:16:44,081 --> 00:16:46,719
architecture Thank you for listening in.

251
00:16:46,786 --> 00:16:50,830
I am Prateek Jindal, and this
was Building Self-Healing Cloud

252
00:16:50,831 --> 00:16:53,057
Infrastructure with Feature Flags.

253
00:16:53,430 --> 00:16:54,101
Have a good day.

